anydoc.wasm is a build artifact, committed on purpose.

Go modules have no build step: whatever is in the repository is what `go get`
delivers to downstream users. So the module is built once, reviewed, and
committed, rather than produced on each install.

  what      wasm32-wasip1 command module
  source    rust/src/main.rs, wrapping the anydoc crate pinned in rust/Cargo.toml
  built by  `make wasm` (Rust 1.88 + wasm32-wasip1, then wasm-opt -Oz)
            or the build-wasm workflow, which opens a PR
  checksum  anydoc.wasm.sha256
  version   embed.go's EmbeddedAnydocVersion, reported by anydoc.Info()

Rebuilding it changes conversion output for every downstream user, which is why
the crate version is pinned with `=` and bumping it goes through a reviewed PR.

To develop against the harness without a Rust toolchain, use `make test`: it
builds a Go wasip1 stub speaking the same ABI and runs the suite under
-tags anydoc_nowasm.
